#gene/RAB39B","cell_types":[{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_symbol":"RAB39B","gene_symbol_current":"RAB39B","gene_id":"ENSG00000155961","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.0713808,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0745469,"nucleus_score":-2.01346,"pct_ad":0.126879,"pct_control":0.136625,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.994258,"donor_log2fc":-0.0343079,"donor_lfcse":0.141624,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-1.78514e-06}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000155961","entrez_id":["116442"],"uniprot":["Q96DA2"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes a member of the Rab family of proteins. Rab proteins are small GTPases that are involved in vesicular trafficking. Mutations in this gene are associated with X-linked cognitive disability. [provided by RefSeq, Aug 2013].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q96DA2","text":"The small GTPases Rab are key regulators of intracellular membrane trafficking, from the formation of transport vesicles to their fusion with membranes. Rabs cycle between an inactive GDP-bound form and an active GTP-bound form that is able to recruit to membranes different sets of downstream effectors directly responsible for vesicle formation, movement, tethering and fusion (PubMed:27103069). RAB39B is involved in autophagy and may function in autophagosome formation (PubMed:27103069, PubMed:37821429). Binds downstream effector PICK1 to ensure selectively GRIA2 exit from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi and to regulate AMPAR composition at the post-synapses and thus synaptic transmission (By similarity). May regulate the homeostasis of SNCA/alpha-synuclein (By similarity)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}